    <DataNode elementId="CPD-8997" textLabel="(3S)-linalool" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="CDOSHBSSFJOMGT-SNVBAGLBSA-N" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="200" centerY="500" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment>|FRAME: Linalools Linalool| (3,7-dimethyl-1,6-octadien-3-ol) is an acyclic monoterpene alcohol with a pleasant fragrance associated with a diverse array of plants |CITS: [7522061][RAGUSO]|. It is found in lavender, bergamot, jasmine, basil, rosemary, sage, star-anise, cinnamon, clove, nutmeg, coriander, cardamom, ginger, black pepper and mandarin, and is one of the three terpene compounds principally responsible for Muscat aroma. Linalool is a primary compound in floral scent research. Both enantiomeric forms, |FRAME: CPD-8996| and |FRAME: CPD-8997|, occur in nature. Linalool is involved as an attractant in plant interactions with pollinators, herbivores and parasitoids |CITS: [RAGUSO]|. It has also been shown to be induced by artificial wounding and by jasmonic acid |CITS: [17440821]|. The &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; synthesis of linalool has been demonstrated in floral tissues |CITS: [17440821]|, barks and roots |CITS: [RAGUSO]|. Junipers, dicots and monocots emit linalool along with other monoterpenoids |CITS: [RAGUSO]|. Even though fungi typically do not produce monoterpenes, the &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; synthesis of linalool was demonstrate in some fungi, including the wine yeast strain, &lt;i&gt;Saccharomyces cerevisiae&lt;/i&gt; M522, in the absence of grape derived precursors |CITS: [15668008]|.</Comment>
